{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788348082144,"sessionID":"ses_f9e260b29ffeaMqkDKkEFk3yuW","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_c9ee436c5d9d4516ae8caa71","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/masked_scale-generate/task.md"},"output":"<path>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/masked_scale-generate/task.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: You are implementing ONE operator for the CANN Bench benchmark (https://gitcode.com/cann/cann-bench) as a pyasc asc2 JIT kernel module targeting an Ascend 950PR NPU. You have NO NPU access — the official harness evaluates your file on real hardware after you finish. Score per operator = 0.2 compile + 0.3 accuracy (all 20 cases must pass a relative-error check against golden.py) + 0.5 performance (profiler-measured kernel time vs an aclnn baseline).\n2: \n3: # Task\n4: \n5: Operator: **MaskedScale**. Write the file `candidate.py` in the current working directory: a complete Python module whose public callable `masked_scale` implements the schema below. Nothing else — no tests, no other files.\n6: \n7: # Operator specification\n8: \n9: ## desc.md\n10: \n11: # MaskedScale 算子 API 描述\n12: \n13: ## 1. 算子简介\n14: \n15: MaskedScale 算子对输入张量进行掩码缩放操作，支持输入张量 x 和掩码张量 mask 的不同数据类型组合。\n16: \n17: **主要应用场景**：\n18: - Transformer 中的注意力掩码缩放\n19: - Dropout 的掩码乘法实现\n20: - 条件计算中的选择性缩放\n21: \n22: **算子特征**：\n23: - 难度等级：L1（MaskPredicate）\n24: - 双输入单输出，逐元素运算，输入 x、mask 和输出 y 的 shape 需一致\n25: \n26: ## 2. 算子定义\n27: \n28: ### 数学公式\n29: \n30: $$\n31: y = x \\cdot mask \\cdot scale\n32: $$\n33: \n34: ## 3. 接口规范\n35: \n36: ### 算子原型\n37: \n38: ```python\n39: cann_bench.masked_scale(Tensor x, Tensor mask, float scale=1.0) -> Tensor y\n40: ```\n41: \n42: ### 输入参数说明\n43: \n44: | 参数 | 类型 | 默认值 | 描述 |\n45: |------|------|--------|------|\n46: | x | Tensor | 必选 | 输入张量 |\n47: | mask | Tensor | 必选 | 掩码张量，shape 须与 x 一致 |\n48: | scale | float | 1.0 | 缩放因子 |\n49: \n50: ### 输出\n51: \n52: | 参数 | Shape | dtype | 描述 |\n53: |------|-------|-------|------|\n54: | y | 与输入 x 相同 | 与输入 x 相同 | 掩码缩放结果 |\n55: \n56: ### 数据类型\n57: \n58: x 与 mask 支持不同 dtype 的组合：\n59: \n60: | x dtype | mask dtype | y dtype |\n61: |---------|-----------|---------|\n62: | float16 | int8 / uint8 / float16 / bfloat16 / float32 | float16 |\n63: | bfloat16 | int8 / uint8 / float16 / bfloat16 / float32 | bfloat16 |\n64: | float32 | int8 / uint8 / float16 / bfloat16 / float32 | float32 |\n65: \n66: ### 规则与约束\n67: \n68: - 输入 x、mask 的 shape 必须完全一致，输出 y 的 shape 也与之相同\n69: - 输出 dtype 与输入 x 的 dtype 一致\n70: - mask 通常取值 0 或 1，但不限于此（也支持连续值掩码）\n71: \n72: ### 支持范围\n73: \n74: 输入 tensor 各维度与参数的支持范围：\n75: \n76: | 维度 / 参数 | 范围 | 备注 |\n77: |---|---|---|\n78: | `x` / `mask` 维度数 | 1 ~ 8 | cases.csv 实测 1D ~ 5D；mask 与 x 同 shape |\n79: | `x` / `mask` 各维大小 | 1 ~ 1048576 | cases.csv 各维实测 2 ~ 1000007 |\n80: | `x` / `mask` 元素总数 | 1 ~ 64M | cases.csv 实测 ~1M ~ ~64M |\n81: | `scale` | -1024.0 ~ 1024.0 | cases.csv 实测 -1.0 ~ 10.0（含 inf / nan 特殊值） |\n82: \n83: 约束：`mask` 与 `x` shape 必须完全一致；输出 dtype 与 `x` dtype 相同。\n84: \n85: ## 4. 精度要求\n86: \n87: 采用[生态算子精度标准](https://gitcode.com/cann/opbase/blob/master/docs/zh/ops_precision_standard/experimental_standard.md)进行验证。\n88: \n89: **误差指标**：\n90: \n91: 1. 平均相对误差（MERE）：采样点中相对误差平均值\n92: \n93:    $$\n94:    \\text{MERE} = \\text{avg}(\\frac{\\text{abs}(actual - golden)}{\\text{abs}(golden)+\\text{1e-7}})\n95:    $$\n96: \n97: 2. 最大相对误差（MARE）：采样点中相对误差最大值\n98: \n99:    $$\n100:    \\text{MARE} = \\max(\\frac{\\text{abs}(actual - golden)}{\\text{abs}(golden)+\\text{1e-7}})\n101:    $$\n102: \n103: **通过标准**：\n104: \n105: | 数据类型 | FLOAT16 | BFLOAT16 | FLOAT32 | HiFLOAT32 | FLOAT8 E4M3 | FLOAT8 E5M2 |\n106: |----------|---------|----------|---------|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n107: | **通过阈值(Threshold)** | 2^-10 | 2^-7 | 2^-13 | 2^-11 | 2^-3 | 2^-2 |\n108: \n109: 当平均相对误差 MERE < Threshold，最大相对误差 MARE < 10 * Threshold 时判定为通过。\n110: \n111: \n112: ## 5. 标准 Golden 代码\n113: \n114: ```python\n115: import torch\n116: \n117: def masked_scale(\n118:     x: torch.Tensor, mask: torch.Tensor, scale: float = 1.0\n119: ) -> torch.Tensor:\n120:     \"\"\"\n121:     对输入张量进行掩码缩放，支持x和mask的不同数据类型组合\n122: \n123:     公式: y = x * mask * scale\n124: \n125:     Args:\n126:         x: 输入张量\n127:         mask: 掩码张量\n128:         scale: 缩放因子\n129: \n130:     Returns:\n131:         掩码缩放结果\n132:     \"\"\"\n133: \n134:     y = x * mask * scale\n135:     return y.to(x.dtype)\n136: ```\n137: \n138: ## 6. 额外信息\n139: \n140: ### 算子调用示例\n141: \n142: ```python\n143: import torch\n144: import cann_bench\n145: \n146: x = torch.randn(1024, 1024, dtype=torch.float16, device=\"npu\")\n147: mask = torch.randint(0, 2, (1024, 1024), dtype=torch.int8, device=\"npu\")\n148: y = cann_bench.masked_scale(x, mask, scale=2.0)\n149: ```\n150: \n151: ## proto.yaml\n152: \n153: ```yaml\n154: operator:\n155:   name: MaskedScale\n156:   category: MaskPredicate\n157:   difficulty: L1\n158:   formula: y = x * mask * scale\n159:   description: 对输入张量进行掩码缩放，支持x和mask的不同数据类型组合\n160:   shape_support: 输入x、mask、输出y的shape需一致\n161:   attrs:\n162:   - name: scale\n163:     type: float\n164:     default: 1.0\n165:     description: 缩放因子\n166:     required: false\n167:   inputs:\n168:   - name: x\n169:     description: 输入张量\n170:     dtype:\n171:     - float16\n172:     - bfloat16\n173:     - float32\n174:   - name: mask\n175:     description: 掩码张量\n176:     dtype:\n177:     - int8\n178:     - uint8\n179:     - float16\n180:     - bfloat16\n181:     - float32\n182:   outputs:\n183:   - name: y\n184:     description: 掩码缩放结果\n185:     dtype:\n186:     - float16\n187:     - bfloat16\n188:     - float32\n189:   schema: masked_scale(Tensor x, Tensor mask, float scale=1.0) -> Tensor y\n190: ```\n191: \n192: ## golden.py — reference semantics ONLY (you must NOT compute with torch like this; reimplement the math in asc2 kernels)\n193: \n194: ```python\n195: #!/usr/bin/python3\n196: # coding=utf-8\n197: \n198: \n199: import torch\n200: \n201: \"\"\"\n202: MaskedScale算子Torch Golden参考实现\n203: \n204: 对输入张量进行掩码缩放，支持x和mask的不同数据类型组合\n205: 公式: y = x * mask * scale\n206: \"\"\"\n207: def masked_scale(\n208:     x: torch.Tensor, mask: torch.Tensor, scale: float = 1.0\n209: ) -> torch.Tensor:\n210:     \"\"\"\n211:     对输入张量进行掩码缩放，支持x和mask的不同数据类型组合\n212:     \n213:     公式: y = x * mask * scale\n214:     \n215:     Args:\n216:         x: 输入张量\n217:         mask: 掩码张量\n218:         scale: 缩放因子\n219:     \n220:     Returns:\n221:         掩码缩放结果\n222:     \"\"\"\n223: \n224:     y = x * mask * scale\n225:     return y.to(x.dtype)\n226: ```\n227: \n228: ## Evaluation cases your module must handle (shapes, dtypes, value ranges, attrs)\n229: \n230: | case | shapes | dtype | value_range | attrs |\n231: |---|---|---|---|---|\n232: | 1 | [[1024, 1024], [1024, 1024]] | ['float16', 'int8'] | [[-1, 1], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 1.0} |\n233: | 2 | [[2048, 2048], [2048, 2048]] | ['float32', 'uint8'] | [[-2, 2], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 1.0} |\n234: | 3 | [[4096, 4096], [4096, 4096]] | ['bfloat16', 'float16'] | [[-3, 3], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 1.0} |\n235: | 4 | [[8192, 8192], [8192, 8192]] | ['float16', 'float32'] | [[-10, 10], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 0.5} |\n236: | 5 | [[8192, 8192], [8192, 8192]] | ['float32', 'int8'] | [[-100, 100], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 2.0} |\n237: | 6 | [[1023, 1023], [1023, 1023]] | ['bfloat16', 'uint8'] | [[-0.1, 0.1], [0, 1]] | {'scale': -1.0} |\n238: | 7 | [[1009, 1021], [1009, 1021]] | ['float16', 'int8'] | [[-1, 2], [0, 127]] | {'scale': 0.0} |\n239: | 8 | [[1537, 769], [1537, 769]] | ['float32', 'float16'] | [[-5, 10], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 10.0} |\n240: | 9 | [[363, 367, 373], [363, 367, 373]] | ['bfloat16', 'float32'] | [[-50, 100], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 1.5} |\n241: | 10 | [[2049, 513], [2049, 513]] | ['float16', 'uint8'] | [[-65504, 65504], [0, 255]] | {'scale': 1.0} |\n242: | 11 | [[3, 7, 13, 4003], [3, 7, 13, 4003]] | ['float32', 'int8'] | [[-88, 88], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 1.0} |\n243: | 12 | [[1000007], [1000007]] | ['bfloat16', 'bfloat16'] | [[-inf, inf], [0, 1]] | {'scale': inf} |\n244: | 13 | [[11, 13, 17, 67, 67], [11, 13, 17, 67, 67]] | ['float32', 'float32'] | [[nan, nan], [0, 1]] | {'scale': nan} |\n245: | 14 | [[3, 7, 11, 13, 1013], [3, 7, 11, 13, 1013]] | ['float16', 'int8'] | [[0, 0], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 1.0} |\n246: | 15 | [[512, 2049], [512, 2049]] | ['float32', 'uint8'] | [[-0.5, 0.5], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 1.0} |\n247: | 16 | [[255, 8193], [255, 8193]] | ['bfloat16', 'int8'] | [[-1, 3], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 1.2} |\n248: | 17 | [[4097, 511], [4097, 511]] | ['float16', 'uint8'] | [[-1000, 1000], [0, 1]] | {'scale': -0.5} |\n249: | 18 | [[2, 511, 2049], [2, 511, 2049]] | ['float32', 'float16'] | [[-0.2, 0.2], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 0.75} |\n250: | 19 | [[4, 255, 2049], [4, 255, 2049]] | ['bfloat16', 'float32'] | [[-3, 6], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 3.0} |\n251: | 20 | [[2, 3, 17, 1024, 101], [2, 3, 17, 1024, 101]] | ['float32', 'int8'] | [[-20, 40], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 1.0} |\n252: \n253: # Reference module — sigmoid.py from this submission (structure to copy; it scores 100% accuracy on this harness)\n254: \n255: ```python\n256: \"\"\"CANN Bench Sigmoid interface implemented as a pyasc asc2 kernel.\n257: \n258: Kernel design (pyasc-api-patterns Pattern A, grid-stride variant):\n259:   - 1-D flatten; each block strides over tiles so any element count works.\n260:   - Tail tiles handled with ``real_shape`` loads/stores (no host padding).\n261:   - f16/bf16 inputs are promoted to f32 inside the kernel for precision\n262:     (the spec's precision standard expects f32 internal compute).\n263:   - Host selects between two compiled tile sizes: a wide tile (3072) when\n264:     the element count fills all 72 cores, otherwise a narrow tile (1024) to\n265:     maximize core utilization on small shapes.\n266:   - sigmoid(x) = 1 / (1 + e^(-x)); IEEE saturation gives the correct\n267:     limits at extreme inputs (e^inf -> inf -> y=0, e^-inf -> 0 -> y=1).\n268: \"\"\"\n269: \n270: import torch\n271: \n272: import asc\n273: import asc2\n274: \n275: from ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform\n276: \n277: _WIDE_TILE = 3072\n278: _NARROW_TILE = 1024\n279: _MAX_CORES = 72\n280: \n281: \n282: @asc2.jit\n283: def _sigmoid_kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress,\n284:                     size: int, num_tiles: int, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n285:     x_gm = asc2.global_tensor(x_ptr, [size])\n286:     out_gm = asc2.global_tensor(out_ptr, [size])\n287:     for t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(),\n288:                         unroll_factor=2):\n289:         off = t * tile_size\n290:         n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off\n291:         x = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n292:         xf = x.to(asc.float32)\n293:         y = asc2.div(1.0, asc2.exp(-xf) + 1.0)\n294:         asc2.copy_out(y.to(x.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n295: \n296: \n297: def sigmoid(x: torch.Tensor) -> torch.Tensor:\n298:     \"\"\"Element-wise sigmoid of an NPU tensor via a pyasc asc2 kernel.\"\"\"\n299:     ensure_npu_platform()\n300:     if not x.is_contiguous():\n301:         x = x.contiguous()\n302:     out = torch.empty_like(x)\n303:     size = x.numel()\n304:     if size == 0:\n305:         return out\n306:     if size >= _MAX_CORES * _WIDE_TILE:\n307:         num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _WIDE_TILE)\n308:         cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n309:         _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _WIDE_TILE)\n310:     else:\n311:         num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _NARROW_TILE)\n312:         cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n313:         _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _NARROW_TILE)\n314:     return out\n315: \n316: ```\n317: \n318: # pyasc asc2 kernel contract (follow EXACTLY — every rule below was learned from real failures on this hardware)\n319: \n320: ## Module shape\n321: \n322: Your file becomes `cann_bench/<module>.py` inside the submission wheel. It must contain:\n323: \n324: - imports at module top: `import torch`, `import asc`, `import asc2`,\n325:   `from ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform` (and `import math` if needed)\n326: - one or more `@asc2.jit` kernel functions\n327: - ONE public callable matching the operator schema exactly (name and signature)\n328: - wrapper body: call `ensure_npu_platform()` first; make inputs contiguous if\n329:   needed (`x = x.contiguous()` is allowed); allocate outputs with\n330:   `torch.empty_like(x)` or `torch.empty(shape, dtype=..., device=x.device)`;\n331:   launch `kernel[cores](tensor_args..., int_args..., float_args..., constexpr_args...)`;\n332:   return contiguous NPU tensor(s)\n333: \n334: ## Kernel authoring rules\n335: \n336: - Global memory views: `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [size])` (1-D) or\n337:   `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [rows, cols])` (2-D). Ranks of global_tensor /\n338:   copy_in / copy_out / offsets must ALL match — never mix 1-D and 2-D.\n339: - Kernel params: pointers typed `asc.GlobalAddress`; sizes as plain `int`\n340:   (runtime); tile sizes as `asc.ConstExpr[int]` (compile-time; REQUIRED for any\n341:   value used inside a copy_in tile shape); scalars as `float`.\n342: - Grid-stride tile loop (the proven pattern):\n343: \n344: ```python\n345: for t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(), unroll_factor=2):\n346:     off = t * tile_size\n347:     n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off   # tail handling\n348:     x = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n349:     ...compute on tiles...\n350:     asc2.copy_out(y.to(x.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n351: ```\n352: \n353: - Launch: `kernel[cores](...)` with `cores = min(72, num_tiles)` (72 AIV cores\n354:   on this 950PR box). No stream argument.\n355: - Available tile ops: `+ - * /` (tile-tile and tile-scalar), `asc2.abs`,\n356:   `asc2.exp`, `asc2.exp2`, `asc2.log`, `asc2.log2`, `asc2.sqrt`,\n357:   `asc2.rsqrt`, `asc2.tanh`, `asc2.erf`, `asc2.sin`, `asc2.cos`,\n358:   `asc2.floor`, `asc2.ceil`, `asc2.relu`, `asc2.maximum`, `asc2.minimum`,\n359:   comparisons (`x >= 0.0`, `asc2.less(a, b)`, ... — NO int64 operands),\n360:   `asc2.where(cond, a, b)`, `asc2.reduce_sum(x)`, `asc2.reduce_max(x)`,\n361:   `asc2.reduce_min(x)`, `asc2.full([shape], scalar, dtype=...)`,\n362:   `asc2.cast(tile, dtype)` / `tile.to(dtype)` casts, integer\n363:   `asc2.left_shift`/`asc2.right_shift`, tile-shape ops `asc2.reshape`,\n364:   `asc2.transpose`, `asc2.ravel`, `asc2.expand_dims`, `asc2.squeeze`,\n365:   `asc2.broadcast_to`, `asc2.concat`, unary `-x`.\n366: - int8 tiles: loading (copy_in) is fine but NO vector op accepts int8 input\n367:   (not even `.to`); convert with `asc2.cast(t, asc.float16)` first. There is\n368:   no uint8 tile dtype at all.\n369: - Scalars go on the RIGHT of tile arithmetic (Tile has no `__rmul__`):\n370:   write `x * 0.5`, NEVER `0.5 * x`. Same for `+ - /`.\n371: - f16/bf16 inputs: promote to f32 in-kernel (`xf = x.to(asc.float32)`),\n372:   compute in f32, cast back on copy_out (`y.to(x.dtype)`).\n373: - UB (unified buffer) budget: ~253952 bytes total under static allocation.\n374:   Every distinct f32 tile value costs `4 * TILE` bytes, `unroll_factor=2`\n375:   doubles the total, and the compiler adds hidden temporaries — MEASURED\n376:   calibration: the sigmoid chain (f16 load, f32 cast, `-x`, `exp`, `+1`,\n377:   `div`, f16 store ≈ 6 visible values) uses 155648 bytes at TILE=2048 and\n378:   311296 (OVERFLOW) at TILE=4096, i.e. real usage ≈ 1.6x the naive\n379:   `visible_values * 4 * TILE * 2` estimate. Budget with that 1.6x factor.\n380:   Rule of thumb: TILE=2048 for short chains (< 8 values), 1024 for medium,\n381:   512 for long (> 16). A launch failing with `RuntimeError: UB overflow: X\n382:   bytes are available, Y bytes are used` means: halve TILE (do NOT drop\n383:   cases).\n384: - `asc2.where` / comparison destination tiles must be a multiple of 256 bytes\n385:   (`TILE * 4 % 256 == 0` for f32 — any TILE >= 64 is safe).\n386: - Loop-carried scalar accumulators (VERIFIED on this build): seed with\n387:   `acc = asc2.reduce_sum(asc2.full([1, 64], 0.0, dtype=asc.float32))`\n388:   (a bare `acc = 0.0` fails codegen with \"re-assigned to an object with\n389:   different type\"), then `acc = acc + asc2.reduce_sum(x)` inside a plain\n390:   `asc2.range(...)` loop. `asc2.range` accepts ONLY `unroll_factor` and\n391:   `parallel` — there is NO `gm_barrier` kwarg on this build (it raises\n392:   TypeError).\n393: - Cross-core reductions (VERIFIED): `asc2.atomic_add(src_tile, dst_gm,\n394:   [offset])` atomically accumulates a tile into global memory (dtypes int16/\n395:   int32/f16/bf16/f32; also `asc2.atomic_max`). Host must zero the\n396:   destination first (`torch.zeros(...)` — tensor creation is allowed).\n397:   Pattern: each core reduce_sums its tiles into a scalar, widens it with\n398:   `asc2.full([8], s, dtype=...)`, and atomic_adds slot [0]; a second tiny\n399:   kernel (or the same one on one core) applies any final transform.\n400: - Scalar reduction results must be widened before store:\n401:   `asc2.copy_out(asc2.full([8], s, dtype=...), out_gm, [0], real_shape=[8])`\n402:   style (min 32 bytes).\n403: - Inside `@asc2.jit`: NO `print`, NO imports, NO `break`/`continue`/early\n404:   `return`, NO exceptions, NO Python `range()` over runtime values (use\n405:   `asc2.range`), NO `math.*` calls (precompute module-level constants).\n406: \n407: ## Numerical stability (MANDATORY — f32 cases use ranges like [-88, 88] and [-100, 100] under a ~1.2e-4 relative-error threshold)\n408: \n409: - Never let `exp()` see a positive argument that can overflow; never subtract\n410:   nearly-equal quantities (catastrophic cancellation); never rely on\n411:   `log(1 + tiny)` (flushes to 0 below tiny < 6e-8).\n412: - Proven cancellation-free building blocks (all verified on this harness):\n413:   - `sigmoid(s) = exp(min(s, 0)) / (1 + exp(-|s|))`\n414:   - `1 + tanh(u) = 2 * sigmoid(2u)`\n415:   - `tanh(softplus(x))`: with `w = exp(-|x|)`, equals\n416:     `(1 + 2w) / (1 + 2w + 2w^2)` for `x >= 0`, `(w^2 + 2w) / (w^2 + 2w + 2)`\n417:     for `x < 0` (exact identities; blend with `asc2.where(xf >= 0.0, ...)`)\n418:   - `erfc(z)` for `z >= 0`: Numerical Recipes fit `t * exp(-z*z + P(t))`,\n419:     `t = 1/(1 + z/2)`, rel. err < 1.2e-7 (see the gelu reference module for\n420:     the 9-coefficient Horner chain)\n421: - IEEE special values (inf/nan scalars or extreme inputs) propagate correctly\n422:   through the hardware ops — do NOT special-case them with host branches\n423:   unless the golden does.\n424: \n425: ## Anti-cheat (violations zero the submission)\n426: \n427: - ALL numerical work happens inside `@asc2.jit` kernels launched on the NPU.\n428: - torch usage is allowed ONLY for: output allocation (`torch.empty`,\n429:   `torch.empty_like`), metadata (`.shape`, `.numel()`, `.stride()`, `.dtype`,\n430:   `.is_contiguous()`), contiguity (`.contiguous()`), and views (`.view`,\n431:   `.reshape`, `.narrow`, indexing that returns a view).\n432: - FORBIDDEN anywhere in the module: torch math/compute ops (`torch.mul`,\n433:   `torch.norm`, `torch.nn.functional.*`, tensor arithmetic like `a + b`,\n434:   `x.sigmoid()`, `.to(dtype)` casts of device data, `torch.cat`,\n435:   `torch.clone`, `torch.sum`, ...). The harness hooks torch dispatch and\n436:   rotates input data pointers between calls — caching outputs by `data_ptr`\n437:   is detected and scored as cheating.\n438: - Outputs must be contiguous NPU tensors with exactly the golden's\n439:   shape/dtype. Do not return views of inputs.\n440: \n441: \n442: # Operator-specific guidance\n443: \n444: - y = x * mask * scale, elementwise, identical shapes. x dtype is one of\n445:   f16/bf16/f32 and mask dtype INDEPENDENTLY one of int8/uint8/f16/bf16/f32\n446:   (see the cases table). The pyasc JIT specializes per launched-tensor dtype\n447:   automatically, so ONE kernel source covers all combinations.\n448: - float-dtype masks: mf = m.to(asc.float32); y = xf * mf * scale with scale\n449:   as a runtime float kernel argument. Output dtype/shape = x dtype/shape.\n450: - int8/uint8 masks — MEASURED FAILURE to avoid: any vector op directly on an\n451:   int8 tile (including `.to(asc.float32)` and `m * 1.0`) fails with\n452:   \"RuntimeError: 'input' dtype must be one of int16, int32, int64, float16,\n453:   bfloat16, float32, got int8\". The hardware DOES support int8 -> float16\n454:   conversion: first `mh = asc2.cast(m, asc.float16)` (copy_in of the int8\n455:   tile itself is fine), then `mf = mh.to(asc.float32)`. Never touch the int8\n456:   tile with arithmetic.\n457: - torch has no uint8 route into the kernel: on the host reinterpret WITHOUT\n458:   copying via `mask = mask.view(torch.int8)`. Mask bytes can be up to 255\n459:   (case 10), which reinterprets as negative int8 — fix in-kernel after the\n460:   cast: `mf = asc2.where(mf < 0.0, mf + 256.0, mf)`. Apply the +256 fixup\n461:   ONLY in the uint8-origin variant (pass a flag or use a separate kernel);\n462:   int8 masks are genuinely signed and must NOT be fixed up.\n463: - Keep the where destination 256-byte rule in mind for the fixup tile.\n464: - MEASURED pyasc CODEGEN BUG to avoid: a kernel instantiation whose tiles\n465:   include BOTH float16 and bfloat16 types fails to compile with\n466:   \"redefinition of 'c0_f16' with a different type: 'bfloat16_t' vs 'half'\"\n467:   whenever anything beyond a bare cast happens at 16 bits (mask dtype f16\n468:   with x bf16 — cases 3, 6, 16 are the risky combos). VERIFIED-SAFE rule:\n469:   the ONLY operation allowed on a 16-bit tile is a single cast toward f32\n470:   (`m.to(asc.float32)`, or for int8 the hop\n471:   `asc2.cast(m, asc.float16).to(asc.float32)`). No comparisons, no where,\n472:   no arithmetic, no asc2.full at f16/bf16 anywhere in the kernel; do ALL\n473:   logic (including the uint8 +256 fixup) in f32 and cast exactly once on\n474:   copy_out to x's dtype.\n475: \n476: # Deliverable\n477: \n478: Write ONLY `candidate.py` (complete, self-contained module). Public callable named exactly `masked_scale` with the exact schema signature including attr defaults. Think hard about numerical stability across the full value ranges listed above, and about the UB budget for your op chain before picking TILE.\n479: \n480: IMPORTANT — no local execution: pyasc/asc/asc2/torch_npu are NOT installed on this machine and there is no NPU here. Do NOT run, import, compile-check, or test your code, and do not install packages — any such attempt wastes your entire time budget. Reason statically, write `candidate.py`, optionally run `python3 -m py_compile candidate.py` (syntax only), then STOP and reply DONE.\n\n(End of file - total 480 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"You are implementing ONE operator for the CANN Bench benchmark (https://gitcode.com/cann/cann-bench) as a pyasc asc2 JIT kernel module targeting an Ascend 950PR NPU. You have NO NPU access — the official harness evaluates your file on real hardware after you finish. Score per operator = 0.2 compile + 0.3 accuracy (all 20 cases must pass a relative-error check against golden.py) + 0.5 performance (profiler-measured kernel time vs an aclnn baseline).\n\n# Task\n\nOperator: **MaskedScale**. Write the file `candidate.py` in the current working directory: a complete Python module whose public callable `masked_scale` implements the schema below. Nothing else — no tests, no other files.\n\n# Operator specification\n\n## desc.md\n\n# MaskedScale 算子 API 描述\n\n## 1. 算子简介\n\nMaskedScale 算子对输入张量进行掩码缩放操作，支持输入张量 x 和掩码张量 mask 的不同数据类型组合。\n\n**主要应用场景**：\n- Transformer 中的注意力掩码缩放\n- Dropout 的掩码乘法实现\n- 条件计算中的选择性缩放","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/masked_scale-generate/task.md","text":"You are implementing ONE operator for the CANN Bench benchmark (https://gitcode.com/cann/cann-bench) as a pyasc asc2 JIT kernel module targeting an Ascend 950PR NPU. You have NO NPU access — the official harness evaluates your file on real hardware after you finish. Score per operator = 0.2 compile + 0.3 accuracy (all 20 cases must pass a relative-error check against golden.py) + 0.5 performance (profiler-measured kernel time vs an aclnn baseline).\n\n# Task\n\nOperator: **MaskedScale**. Write the file `candidate.py` in the current working directory: a complete Python module whose public callable `masked_scale` implements the schema below. Nothing else — no tests, no other files.\n\n# Operator specification\n\n## desc.md\n\n# MaskedScale 算子 API 描述\n\n## 1. 算子简介\n\nMaskedScale 算子对输入张量进行掩码缩放操作，支持输入张量 x 和掩码张量 mask 的不同数据类型组合。\n\n**主要应用场景**：\n- Transformer 中的注意力掩码缩放\n- Dropout 的掩码乘法实现\n- 条件计算中的选择性缩放\n\n**算子特征**：\n- 难度等级：L1（MaskPredicate）\n- 双输入单输出，逐元素运算，输入 x、mask 和输出 y 的 shape 需一致\n\n## 2. 算子定义\n\n### 数学公式\n\n$$\ny = x \\cdot mask \\cdot scale\n$$\n\n## 3. 接口规范\n\n### 算子原型\n\n```python\ncann_bench.masked_scale(Tensor x, Tensor mask, float scale=1.0) -> Tensor y\n```\n\n### 输入参数说明\n\n| 参数 | 类型 | 默认值 | 描述 |\n|------|------|--------|------|\n| x | Tensor | 必选 | 输入张量 |\n| mask | Tensor | 必选 | 掩码张量，shape 须与 x 一致 |\n| scale | float | 1.0 | 缩放因子 |\n\n### 输出\n\n| 参数 | Shape | dtype | 描述 |\n|------|-------|-------|------|\n| y | 与输入 x 相同 | 与输入 x 相同 | 掩码缩放结果 |\n\n### 数据类型\n\nx 与 mask 支持不同 dtype 的组合：\n\n| x dtype | mask dtype | y dtype |\n|---------|-----------|---------|\n| float16 | int8 / uint8 / float16 / bfloat16 / float32 | float16 |\n| bfloat16 | int8 / uint8 / float16 / bfloat16 / float32 | bfloat16 |\n| float32 | int8 / uint8 / float16 / bfloat16 / float32 | float32 |\n\n### 规则与约束\n\n- 输入 x、mask 的 shape 必须完全一致，输出 y 的 shape 也与之相同\n- 输出 dtype 与输入 x 的 dtype 一致\n- mask 通常取值 0 或 1，但不限于此（也支持连续值掩码）\n\n### 支持范围\n\n输入 tensor 各维度与参数的支持范围：\n\n| 维度 / 参数 | 范围 | 备注 |\n|---|---|---|\n| `x` / `mask` 维度数 | 1 ~ 8 | cases.csv 实测 1D ~ 5D；mask 与 x 同 shape |\n| `x` / `mask` 各维大小 | 1 ~ 1048576 | cases.csv 各维实测 2 ~ 1000007 |\n| `x` / `mask` 元素总数 | 1 ~ 64M | cases.csv 实测 ~1M ~ ~64M |\n| `scale` | -1024.0 ~ 1024.0 | cases.csv 实测 -1.0 ~ 10.0（含 inf / nan 特殊值） |\n\n约束：`mask` 与 `x` shape 必须完全一致；输出 dtype 与 `x` dtype 相同。\n\n## 4. 精度要求\n\n采用[生态算子精度标准](https://gitcode.com/cann/opbase/blob/master/docs/zh/ops_precision_standard/experimental_standard.md)进行验证。\n\n**误差指标**：\n\n1. 平均相对误差（MERE）：采样点中相对误差平均值\n\n   $$\n   \\text{MERE} = \\text{avg}(\\frac{\\text{abs}(actual - golden)}{\\text{abs}(golden)+\\text{1e-7}})\n   $$\n\n2. 最大相对误差（MARE）：采样点中相对误差最大值\n\n   $$\n   \\text{MARE} = \\max(\\frac{\\text{abs}(actual - golden)}{\\text{abs}(golden)+\\text{1e-7}})\n   $$\n\n**通过标准**：\n\n| 数据类型 | FLOAT16 | BFLOAT16 | FLOAT32 | HiFLOAT32 | FLOAT8 E4M3 | FLOAT8 E5M2 |\n|----------|---------|----------|---------|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| **通过阈值(Threshold)** | 2^-10 | 2^-7 | 2^-13 | 2^-11 | 2^-3 | 2^-2 |\n\n当平均相对误差 MERE < Threshold，最大相对误差 MARE < 10 * Threshold 时判定为通过。\n\n\n## 5. 标准 Golden 代码\n\n```python\nimport torch\n\ndef masked_scale(\n    x: torch.Tensor, mask: torch.Tensor, scale: float = 1.0\n) -> torch.Tensor:\n    \"\"\"\n    对输入张量进行掩码缩放，支持x和mask的不同数据类型组合\n\n    公式: y = x * mask * scale\n\n    Args:\n        x: 输入张量\n        mask: 掩码张量\n        scale: 缩放因子\n\n    Returns:\n        掩码缩放结果\n    \"\"\"\n\n    y = x * mask * scale\n    return y.to(x.dtype)\n```\n\n## 6. 额外信息\n\n### 算子调用示例\n\n```python\nimport torch\nimport cann_bench\n\nx = torch.randn(1024, 1024, dtype=torch.float16, device=\"npu\")\nmask = torch.randint(0, 2, (1024, 1024), dtype=torch.int8, device=\"npu\")\ny = cann_bench.masked_scale(x, mask, scale=2.0)\n```\n\n## proto.yaml\n\n```yaml\noperator:\n  name: MaskedScale\n  category: MaskPredicate\n  difficulty: L1\n  formula: y = x * mask * scale\n  description: 对输入张量进行掩码缩放，支持x和mask的不同数据类型组合\n  shape_support: 输入x、mask、输出y的shape需一致\n  attrs:\n  - name: scale\n    type: float\n    default: 1.0\n    description: 缩放因子\n    required: false\n  inputs:\n  - name: x\n    description: 输入张量\n    dtype:\n    - float16\n    - bfloat16\n    - float32\n  - name: mask\n    description: 掩码张量\n    dtype:\n    - int8\n    - uint8\n    - float16\n    - bfloat16\n    - float32\n  outputs:\n  - name: y\n    description: 掩码缩放结果\n    dtype:\n    - float16\n    - bfloat16\n    - float32\n  schema: masked_scale(Tensor x, Tensor mask, float scale=1.0) -> Tensor y\n```\n\n## golden.py — reference semantics ONLY (you must NOT compute with torch like this; reimplement the math in asc2 kernels)\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/python3\n# coding=utf-8\n\n\nimport torch\n\n\"\"\"\nMaskedScale算子Torch Golden参考实现\n\n对输入张量进行掩码缩放，支持x和mask的不同数据类型组合\n公式: y = x * mask * scale\n\"\"\"\ndef masked_scale(\n    x: torch.Tensor, mask: torch.Tensor, scale: float = 1.0\n) -> torch.Tensor:\n    \"\"\"\n    对输入张量进行掩码缩放，支持x和mask的不同数据类型组合\n    \n    公式: y = x * mask * scale\n    \n    Args:\n        x: 输入张量\n        mask: 掩码张量\n        scale: 缩放因子\n    \n    Returns:\n        掩码缩放结果\n    \"\"\"\n\n    y = x * mask * scale\n    return y.to(x.dtype)\n```\n\n## Evaluation cases your module must handle (shapes, dtypes, value ranges, attrs)\n\n| case | shapes | dtype | value_range | attrs |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | [[1024, 1024], [1024, 1024]] | ['float16', 'int8'] | [[-1, 1], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 1.0} |\n| 2 | [[2048, 2048], [2048, 2048]] | ['float32', 'uint8'] | [[-2, 2], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 1.0} |\n| 3 | [[4096, 4096], [4096, 4096]] | ['bfloat16', 'float16'] | [[-3, 3], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 1.0} |\n| 4 | [[8192, 8192], [8192, 8192]] | ['float16', 'float32'] | [[-10, 10], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 0.5} |\n| 5 | [[8192, 8192], [8192, 8192]] | ['float32', 'int8'] | [[-100, 100], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 2.0} |\n| 6 | [[1023, 1023], [1023, 1023]] | ['bfloat16', 'uint8'] | [[-0.1, 0.1], [0, 1]] | {'scale': -1.0} |\n| 7 | [[1009, 1021], [1009, 1021]] | ['float16', 'int8'] | [[-1, 2], [0, 127]] | {'scale': 0.0} |\n| 8 | [[1537, 769], [1537, 769]] | ['float32', 'float16'] | [[-5, 10], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 10.0} |\n| 9 | [[363, 367, 373], [363, 367, 373]] | ['bfloat16', 'float32'] | [[-50, 100], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 1.5} |\n| 10 | [[2049, 513], [2049, 513]] | ['float16', 'uint8'] | [[-65504, 65504], [0, 255]] | {'scale': 1.0} |\n| 11 | [[3, 7, 13, 4003], [3, 7, 13, 4003]] | ['float32', 'int8'] | [[-88, 88], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 1.0} |\n| 12 | [[1000007], [1000007]] | ['bfloat16', 'bfloat16'] | [[-inf, inf], [0, 1]] | {'scale': inf} |\n| 13 | [[11, 13, 17, 67, 67], [11, 13, 17, 67, 67]] | ['float32', 'float32'] | [[nan, nan], [0, 1]] | {'scale': nan} |\n| 14 | [[3, 7, 11, 13, 1013], [3, 7, 11, 13, 1013]] | ['float16', 'int8'] | [[0, 0], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 1.0} |\n| 15 | [[512, 2049], [512, 2049]] | ['float32', 'uint8'] | [[-0.5, 0.5], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 1.0} |\n| 16 | [[255, 8193], [255, 8193]] | ['bfloat16', 'int8'] | [[-1, 3], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 1.2} |\n| 17 | [[4097, 511], [4097, 511]] | ['float16', 'uint8'] | [[-1000, 1000], [0, 1]] | {'scale': -0.5} |\n| 18 | [[2, 511, 2049], [2, 511, 2049]] | ['float32', 'float16'] | [[-0.2, 0.2], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 0.75} |\n| 19 | [[4, 255, 2049], [4, 255, 2049]] | ['bfloat16', 'float32'] | [[-3, 6], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 3.0} |\n| 20 | [[2, 3, 17, 1024, 101], [2, 3, 17, 1024, 101]] | ['float32', 'int8'] | [[-20, 40], [0, 1]] | {'scale': 1.0} |\n\n# Reference module — sigmoid.py from this submission (structure to copy; it scores 100% accuracy on this harness)\n\n```python\n\"\"\"CANN Bench Sigmoid interface implemented as a pyasc asc2 kernel.\n\nKernel design (pyasc-api-patterns Pattern A, grid-stride variant):\n  - 1-D flatten; each block strides over tiles so any element count works.\n  - Tail tiles handled with ``real_shape`` loads/stores (no host padding).\n  - f16/bf16 inputs are promoted to f32 inside the kernel for precision\n    (the spec's precision standard expects f32 internal compute).\n  - Host selects between two compiled tile sizes: a wide tile (3072) when\n    the element count fills all 72 cores, otherwise a narrow tile (1024) to\n    maximize core utilization on small shapes.\n  - sigmoid(x) = 1 / (1 + e^(-x)); IEEE saturation gives the correct\n    limits at extreme inputs (e^inf -> inf -> y=0, e^-inf -> 0 -> y=1).\n\"\"\"\n\nimport torch\n\nimport asc\nimport asc2\n\nfrom ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform\n\n_WIDE_TILE = 3072\n_NARROW_TILE = 1024\n_MAX_CORES = 72\n\n\n@asc2.jit\ndef _sigmoid_kernel(x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, out_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress,\n                    size: int, num_tiles: int, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int]):\n    x_gm = asc2.global_tensor(x_ptr, [size])\n    out_gm = asc2.global_tensor(out_ptr, [size])\n    for t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(),\n                        unroll_factor=2):\n        off = t * tile_size\n        n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off\n        x = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n        xf = x.to(asc.float32)\n        y = asc2.div(1.0, asc2.exp(-xf) + 1.0)\n        asc2.copy_out(y.to(x.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n\n\ndef sigmoid(x: torch.Tensor) -> torch.Tensor:\n    \"\"\"Element-wise sigmoid of an NPU tensor via a pyasc asc2 kernel.\"\"\"\n    ensure_npu_platform()\n    if not x.is_contiguous():\n        x = x.contiguous()\n    out = torch.empty_like(x)\n    size = x.numel()\n    if size == 0:\n        return out\n    if size >= _MAX_CORES * _WIDE_TILE:\n        num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _WIDE_TILE)\n        cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n        _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _WIDE_TILE)\n    else:\n        num_tiles = asc.ceildiv(size, _NARROW_TILE)\n        cores = min(_MAX_CORES, num_tiles)\n        _sigmoid_kernel[cores](x, out, size, num_tiles, _NARROW_TILE)\n    return out\n\n```\n\n# pyasc asc2 kernel contract (follow EXACTLY — every rule below was learned from real failures on this hardware)\n\n## Module shape\n\nYour file becomes `cann_bench/<module>.py` inside the submission wheel. It must contain:\n\n- imports at module top: `import torch`, `import asc`, `import asc2`,\n  `from ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform` (and `import math` if needed)\n- one or more `@asc2.jit` kernel functions\n- ONE public callable matching the operator schema exactly (name and signature)\n- wrapper body: call `ensure_npu_platform()` first; make inputs contiguous if\n  needed (`x = x.contiguous()` is allowed); allocate outputs with\n  `torch.empty_like(x)` or `torch.empty(shape, dtype=..., device=x.device)`;\n  launch `kernel[cores](tensor_args..., int_args..., float_args..., constexpr_args...)`;\n  return contiguous NPU tensor(s)\n\n## Kernel authoring rules\n\n- Global memory views: `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [size])` (1-D) or\n  `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [rows, cols])` (2-D). Ranks of global_tensor /\n  copy_in / copy_out / offsets must ALL match — never mix 1-D and 2-D.\n- Kernel params: pointers typed `asc.GlobalAddress`; sizes as plain `int`\n  (runtime); tile sizes as `asc.ConstExpr[int]` (compile-time; REQUIRED for any\n  value used inside a copy_in tile shape); scalars as `float`.\n- Grid-stride tile loop (the proven pattern):\n\n```python\nfor t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(), unroll_factor=2):\n    off = t * tile_size\n    n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off   # tail handling\n    x = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n    ...compute on tiles...\n    asc2.copy_out(y.to(x.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n```\n\n- Launch: `kernel[cores](...)` with `cores = min(72, num_tiles)` (72 AIV cores\n  on this 950PR box). No stream argument.\n- Available tile ops: `+ - * /` (tile-tile and tile-scalar), `asc2.abs`,\n  `asc2.exp`, `asc2.exp2`, `asc2.log`, `asc2.log2`, `asc2.sqrt`,\n  `asc2.rsqrt`, `asc2.tanh`, `asc2.erf`, `asc2.sin`, `asc2.cos`,\n  `asc2.floor`, `asc2.ceil`, `asc2.relu`, `asc2.maximum`, `asc2.minimum`,\n  comparisons (`x >= 0.0`, `asc2.less(a, b)`, ... — NO int64 operands),\n  `asc2.where(cond, a, b)`, `asc2.reduce_sum(x)`, `asc2.reduce_max(x)`,\n  `asc2.reduce_min(x)`, `asc2.full([shape], scalar, dtype=...)`,\n  `asc2.cast(tile, dtype)` / `tile.to(dtype)` casts, integer\n  `asc2.left_shift`/`asc2.right_shift`, tile-shape ops `asc2.reshape`,\n  `asc2.transpose`, `asc2.ravel`, `asc2.expand_dims`, `asc2.squeeze`,\n  `asc2.broadcast_to`, `asc2.concat`, unary `-x`.\n- int8 tiles: loading (copy_in) is fine but NO vector op accepts int8 input\n  (not even `.to`); convert with `asc2.cast(t, asc.float16)` first. There is\n  no uint8 tile dtype at all.\n- Scalars go on the RIGHT of tile arithmetic (Tile has no `__rmul__`):\n  write `x * 0.5`, NEVER `0.5 * x`. Same for `+ - /`.\n- f16/bf16 inputs: promote to f32 in-kernel (`xf = x.to(asc.float32)`),\n  compute in f32, cast back on copy_out (`y.to(x.dtype)`).\n- UB (unified buffer) budget: ~253952 bytes total under static allocation.\n  Every distinct f32 tile value costs `4 * TILE` bytes, `unroll_factor=2`\n  doubles the total, and the compiler adds hidden temporaries — MEASURED\n  calibration: the sigmoid chain (f16 load, f32 cast, `-x`, `exp`, `+1`,\n  `div`, f16 store ≈ 6 visible values) uses 155648 bytes at TILE=2048 and\n  311296 (OVERFLOW) at TILE=4096, i.e. real usage ≈ 1.6x the naive\n  `visible_values * 4 * TILE * 2` estimate. Budget with that 1.6x factor.\n  Rule of thumb: TILE=2048 for short chains (< 8 values), 1024 for medium,\n  512 for long (> 16). A launch failing with `RuntimeError: UB overflow: X\n  bytes are available, Y bytes are used` means: halve TILE (do NOT drop\n  cases).\n- `asc2.where` / comparison destination tiles must be a multiple of 256 bytes\n  (`TILE * 4 % 256 == 0` for f32 — any TILE >= 64 is safe).\n- Loop-carried scalar accumulators (VERIFIED on this build): seed with\n  `acc = asc2.reduce_sum(asc2.full([1, 64], 0.0, dtype=asc.float32))`\n  (a bare `acc = 0.0` fails codegen with \"re-assigned to an object with\n  different type\"), then `acc = acc + asc2.reduce_sum(x)` inside a plain\n  `asc2.range(...)` loop. `asc2.range` accepts ONLY `unroll_factor` and\n  `parallel` — there is NO `gm_barrier` kwarg on this build (it raises\n  TypeError).\n- Cross-core reductions (VERIFIED): `asc2.atomic_add(src_tile, dst_gm,\n  [offset])` atomically accumulates a tile into global memory (dtypes int16/\n  int32/f16/bf16/f32; also `asc2.atomic_max`). Host must zero the\n  destination first (`torch.zeros(...)` — tensor creation is allowed).\n  Pattern: each core reduce_sums its tiles into a scalar, widens it with\n  `asc2.full([8], s, dtype=...)`, and atomic_adds slot [0]; a second tiny\n  kernel (or the same one on one core) applies any final transform.\n- Scalar reduction results must be widened before store:\n  `asc2.copy_out(asc2.full([8], s, dtype=...), out_gm, [0], real_shape=[8])`\n  style (min 32 bytes).\n- Inside `@asc2.jit`: NO `print`, NO imports, NO `break`/`continue`/early\n  `return`, NO exceptions, NO Python `range()` over runtime values (use\n  `asc2.range`), NO `math.*` calls (precompute module-level constants).\n\n## Numerical stability (MANDATORY — f32 cases use ranges like [-88, 88] and [-100, 100] under a ~1.2e-4 relative-error threshold)\n\n- Never let `exp()` see a positive argument that can overflow; never subtract\n  nearly-equal quantities (catastrophic cancellation); never rely on\n  `log(1 + tiny)` (flushes to 0 below tiny < 6e-8).\n- Proven cancellation-free building blocks (all verified on this harness):\n  - `sigmoid(s) = exp(min(s, 0)) / (1 + exp(-|s|))`\n  - `1 + tanh(u) = 2 * sigmoid(2u)`\n  - `tanh(softplus(x))`: with `w = exp(-|x|)`, equals\n    `(1 + 2w) / (1 + 2w + 2w^2)` for `x >= 0`, `(w^2 + 2w) / (w^2 + 2w + 2)`\n    for `x < 0` (exact identities; blend with `asc2.where(xf >= 0.0, ...)`)\n  - `erfc(z)` for `z >= 0`: Numerical Recipes fit `t * exp(-z*z + P(t))`,\n    `t = 1/(1 + z/2)`, rel. err < 1.2e-7 (see the gelu reference module for\n    the 9-coefficient Horner chain)\n- IEEE special values (inf/nan scalars or extreme inputs) propagate correctly\n  through the hardware ops — do NOT special-case them with host branches\n  unless the golden does.\n\n## Anti-cheat (violations zero the submission)\n\n- ALL numerical work happens inside `@asc2.jit` kernels launched on the NPU.\n- torch usage is allowed ONLY for: output allocation (`torch.empty`,\n  `torch.empty_like`), metadata (`.shape`, `.numel()`, `.stride()`, `.dtype`,\n  `.is_contiguous()`), contiguity (`.contiguous()`), and views (`.view`,\n  `.reshape`, `.narrow`, indexing that returns a view).\n- FORBIDDEN anywhere in the module: torch math/compute ops (`torch.mul`,\n  `torch.norm`, `torch.nn.functional.*`, tensor arithmetic like `a + b`,\n  `x.sigmoid()`, `.to(dtype)` casts of device data, `torch.cat`,\n  `torch.clone`, `torch.sum`, ...). The harness hooks torch dispatch and\n  rotates input data pointers between calls — caching outputs by `data_ptr`\n  is detected and scored as cheating.\n- Outputs must be contiguous NPU tensors with exactly the golden's\n  shape/dtype. Do not return views of inputs.\n\n\n# Operator-specific guidance\n\n- y = x * mask * scale, elementwise, identical shapes. x dtype is one of\n  f16/bf16/f32 and mask dtype INDEPENDENTLY one of int8/uint8/f16/bf16/f32\n  (see the cases table). The pyasc JIT specializes per launched-tensor dtype\n  automatically, so ONE kernel source covers all combinations.\n- float-dtype masks: mf = m.to(asc.float32); y = xf * mf * scale with scale\n  as a runtime float kernel argument. Output dtype/shape = x dtype/shape.\n- int8/uint8 masks — MEASURED FAILURE to avoid: any vector op directly on an\n  int8 tile (including `.to(asc.float32)` and `m * 1.0`) fails with\n  \"RuntimeError: 'input' dtype must be one of int16, int32, int64, float16,\n  bfloat16, float32, got int8\". The hardware DOES support int8 -> float16\n  conversion: first `mh = asc2.cast(m, asc.float16)` (copy_in of the int8\n  tile itself is fine), then `mf = mh.to(asc.float32)`. Never touch the int8\n  tile with arithmetic.\n- torch has no uint8 route into the kernel: on the host reinterpret WITHOUT\n  copying via `mask = mask.view(torch.int8)`. Mask bytes can be up to 255\n  (case 10), which reinterprets as negative int8 — fix in-kernel after the\n  cast: `mf = asc2.where(mf < 0.0, mf + 256.0, mf)`. Apply the +256 fixup\n  ONLY in the uint8-origin variant (pass a flag or use a separate kernel);\n  int8 masks are genuinely signed and must NOT be fixed up.\n- Keep the where destination 256-byte rule in mind for the fixup tile.\n- MEASURED pyasc CODEGEN BUG to avoid: a kernel instantiation whose tiles\n  include BOTH float16 and bfloat16 types fails to compile with\n  \"redefinition of 'c0_f16' with a different type: 'bfloat16_t' vs 'half'\"\n  whenever anything beyond a bare cast happens at 16 bits (mask dtype f16\n  with x bf16 — cases 3, 6, 16 are the risky combos). VERIFIED-SAFE rule:\n  the ONLY operation allowed on a 16-bit tile is a single cast toward f32\n  (`m.to(asc.float32)`, or for int8 the hop\n  `asc2.cast(m, asc.float16).to(asc.float32)`). No comparisons, no where,\n  no arithmetic, no asc2.full at f16/bf16 anywhere in the kernel; do ALL\n  logic (including the uint8 +256 fixup) in f32 and cast exactly once on\n  copy_out to x's dtype.\n\n# Deliverable\n\nWrite ONLY `candidate.py` (complete, self-contained module). Public callable named exactly `masked_scale` with the exact schema signature including attr defaults. Think hard about numerical stability across the full value ranges listed above, and about the UB budget for your op chain before picking TILE.\n\nIMPORTANT — no local execution: pyasc/asc/asc2/torch_npu are NOT installed on this machine and there is no NPU here. Do NOT run, import, compile-check, or test your code, and do not install packages — any such attempt wastes your entire time budget. Reason statically, write `candidate.py`, optionally run `python3 -m py_compile candidate.py` (syntax only), then STOP and reply DONE.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":480,"totalLines":480,"truncated":false}},"title":"integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/masked_scale-generate/task.md","time":{"start":1788348082127,"end":1788348082141}},"id":"prt_061da2657001GUa7CLACgfZP6v","sessionID":"ses_f9e260b29ffeaMqkDKkEFk3yuW","messageID":"msg_061da18a8001RPEMDLA3ofuKvN"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788348082597,"sessionID":"ses_f9e260b29ffeaMqkDKkEFk3yuW","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_70d8da8493f542688d85312d","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/masked_scale-generate/design.md"},"output":"<path>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/masked_scale-generate/design.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Design: MaskedScale pyasc asc2 kernel\n2: \n3: ## Algorithm\n4: \n5: `y = x * mask * scale`, elementwise. Both inputs and the output share the same\n6: shape. Flatten to 1-D on the host (the operation is shape-agnostic). Host\n7: dispatches dtype combos; one kernel source covers all via JIT dtype\n8: specialization.\n9: \n10: ## Pinned-v2 API surface\n11: \n12: | Step | API |\n13: |------|-----|\n14: | Global memory | `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [size])` — 1-D |\n15: | Load tile | `asc2.copy_in(gm, [off], [TILE], real_shape=[n])` |\n16: | Int8 mask promotion | `asc2.cast(m, asc.float16)` then `.to(asc.float32)` |\n17: | Float mask promotion | `m.to(asc.float32)` |\n18: | Compute | `xf * mf * scale` — scalar on right |\n19: | Store | `asc2.copy_out(y.to(x_dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])` |\n20: \n21: ## All 20 cases\n22: \n23: | # | shape (1-D numel) | x dtype | mask dtype | scale | notes |\n24: |---|---|---|---|---|---|\n25: | 1 | 1048576 | f16 | int8 | 1.0 | |\n26: | 2 | 4194304 | f32 | uint8 | 1.0 | uint8 fixup |\n27: | 3 | 16777216 | bf16 | f16 | 1.0 | mixed-16bit: cast mask straight to f32 |\n28: | 4 | 67108864 | f16 | f32 | 0.5 | |\n29: | 5 | 67108864 | f32 | int8 | 2.0 | |\n30: | 6 | 1046529 | bf16 | uint8 | -1.0 | uint8 fixup |\n31: | 7 | 1030189 | f16 | int8 | 0.0 | mask range [0,127] — signed int8, no fixup |\n32: | 8 | 1181953 | f32 | f16 | 10.0 | |\n33: | 9 | 4977117 | bf16 | f32 | 1.5 | |\n34: | 10 | 1049337 | f16 | uint8 | 1.0 | mask [0,255], uint8 fixup |\n35: | 11 | 1093219 | f32 | int8 | 1.0 | 4-D |\n36: | 12 | 1000007 | bf16 | bf16 | inf | 1-D, inf propagation |\n37: | 13 | 10521419 | f32 | f32 | nan | 5-D, nan propagation |\n38: | 14 | 3074517 | f16 | int8 | 1.0 | x all zeros, 5-D |\n39: | 15 | 1049088 | f32 | uint8 | 1.0 | uint8 fixup |\n40: | 16 | 2089215 | bf16 | int8 | 1.2 | |\n41: | 17 | 2093057 | f16 | uint8 | -0.5 | uint8 fixup |\n42: | 18 | 2100222 | f32 | f16 | 0.75 | 3-D |\n43: | 19 | 2099232 | bf16 | f32 | 3.0 | 3-D |\n44: | 20 | 10554624 | f32 | int8 | 1.0 | 5-D |\n45: \n46: uint8 cases: 2, 6, 10, 15, 17 — host `mask.view(torch.int8)`, pass `is_uint8=1`\n47: flag, kernel applies +256 fixup in f32 via `asc2.where`.\n48: \n49: ## Tiling & tails\n50: \n51: - 1-D grid-stride: `for t in asc2.range(block_idx, num_tiles, block_num, unroll_factor=2)`\n52: - Tail: `n = TILE if off + TILE <= size else size - off`; `real_shape=[n]` on\n53:   both copy_in and copy_out.\n54: - Two kernel variants dispatched by host:\n55:   - `_masked_scale_kernel` — normal (int8 or float mask, no uint8 fixup)\n56:   - `_masked_scale_kernel_uint8` — adds `asc2.where(mf < 0.0, mf + 256.0, mf)` after cast\n57: - Both accept `is_uint8` as `asc.ConstExpr[int]`? No — simpler: two separate\n58:   `@asc2.jit` functions to avoid branching cost on every tile.\n59: \n60: ## UB budget\n61: \n62: | f32 tile | bytes per | count | subtotal |\n63: |----------|-----------|-------|----------|\n64: | xf | 4*TILE | 1 | 4T |\n65: | mf (after cast) | 4*TILE | 1 | 4T |\n66: | product xf*mf | 4*TILE | 1 | 4T |\n67: | scaled (xf*mf*scale) | 4*TILE | 1 | 4T |\n68: | where-fixup tile | 4*TILE | 1 (uint8 only) | 4T |\n69: | **visible @ TILE=2048** | | | 32768 (normal) / 40960 (uint8) |\n70: | **x1.6 measured** | | | ~52428 / ~65536 |\n71: | **x2 unroll** | | | ~104857 / ~131072 |\n72: \n73: Budget 253952 B. TILE=2048 fits comfortably for both variants.\n74: \n75: ## Numerical risks\n76: \n77: - **inf/nan** (cases 12, 13): IEEE `*` propagates correctly — no special casing.\n78: - **Zero scale** (case 7): `0.0 * anything = 0.0` in IEEE, even with nan input\n79:   — golden uses same semantics, no issue.\n80: - **Large mask values** (case 10 mask up to 255 via uint8): f32 represents\n81:   exactly, no precision loss.\n82: - **f16/bf16 mixed** (cases 3, 6, 16): both 16-bit tiles are ONLY cast to f32,\n83:   never touched by arithmetic or comparisons at 16-bit.\n84: - **Precision thresholds**: f16 needs MERE < 2^-10 (~9.8e-4), f32 < 2^-13, bf16\n85:   < 2^-7. Single f32 multiply chain is exact for int8/uint8 masks and within\n86:   1 ulp for float masks — well within all thresholds.\n87: \n88: ## Anti-cheat constraints\n89: \n90: - All compute in `@asc2.jit` kernels — no torch arithmetic on tensors.\n91: - torch used only for: `torch.empty_like(x)` allocation, `.numel()`, `.shape`,\n92:   `.dtype`, `.contiguous()`, `.view(torch.int8)` for uint8 reinterpret.\n93: - `ensure_npu_platform()` called first.\n94: - Output is contiguous, same shape and dtype as x.\n95: \n96: ## Local validation ladder\n97: \n98: 1. `python3 -m py_compile candidate.py` — syntax gate.\n99: 2. Worker static contract check — callable signature, imports, anti-cheat.\n100: 3. exact-v2 compile gate: all 20 dtype/constexpr combos must JIT-compile via\n101:    pinned pyasc v2 (`verified-local-compile`).\n102: 4. No numerical claim until camodel or CANNBench evaluation.\n103: \n104: ## Host dispatch pseudocode\n105: \n106: ```\n107: def masked_scale(x, mask, scale=1.0):\n108:     ensure_npu_platform()\n109:     x = x.contiguous(); mask = mask.contiguous()\n110:     out = torch.empty_like(x)\n111:     size = x.numel()\n112:     if size == 0: return out\n113: \n114:     is_uint8 = mask.dtype == torch.uint8\n115:     if is_uint8:\n116:         mask = mask.view(torch.int8)   # no-copy reinterpret\n117: \n118:     TILE = 2048\n119:     num_tiles = ceildiv(size, TILE)\n120:     cores = min(72, num_tiles)\n121: \n122:     if is_uint8:\n123:         _masked_scale_uint8[cores](x, mask, out, size, num_tiles, scale, TILE)\n124:     else:\n125:         _masked_scale_normal[cores](x, mask, out, size, num_tiles, scale, TILE)\n126:     return out\n127: ```\n128: \n129: DESIGN_DONE\n\n(End of file - total 129 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Design: MaskedScale pyasc asc2 kernel\n\n## Algorithm\n\n`y = x * mask * scale`, elementwise. Both inputs and the output share the same\nshape. Flatten to 1-D on the host (the operation is shape-agnostic). Host\ndispatches dtype combos; one kernel source covers all via JIT dtype\nspecialization.\n\n## Pinned-v2 API surface\n\n| Step | API |\n|------|-----|\n| Global memory | `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [size])` — 1-D |\n| Load tile | `asc2.copy_in(gm, [off], [TILE], real_shape=[n])` |\n| Int8 mask promotion | `asc2.cast(m, asc.float16)` then `.to(asc.float32)` |\n| Float mask promotion | `m.to(asc.float32)` |\n| Compute | `xf * mf * scale` — scalar on right |\n| Store | `asc2.copy_out(y.to(x_dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])` |\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/masked_scale-generate/design.md","text":"# Design: MaskedScale pyasc asc2 kernel\n\n## Algorithm\n\n`y = x * mask * scale`, elementwise. Both inputs and the output share the same\nshape. Flatten to 1-D on the host (the operation is shape-agnostic). Host\ndispatches dtype combos; one kernel source covers all via JIT dtype\nspecialization.\n\n## Pinned-v2 API surface\n\n| Step | API |\n|------|-----|\n| Global memory | `asc2.global_tensor(ptr, [size])` — 1-D |\n| Load tile | `asc2.copy_in(gm, [off], [TILE], real_shape=[n])` |\n| Int8 mask promotion | `asc2.cast(m, asc.float16)` then `.to(asc.float32)` |\n| Float mask promotion | `m.to(asc.float32)` |\n| Compute | `xf * mf * scale` — scalar on right |\n| Store | `asc2.copy_out(y.to(x_dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])` |\n\n## All 20 cases\n\n| # | shape (1-D numel) | x dtype | mask dtype | scale | notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 1048576 | f16 | int8 | 1.0 | |\n| 2 | 4194304 | f32 | uint8 | 1.0 | uint8 fixup |\n| 3 | 16777216 | bf16 | f16 | 1.0 | mixed-16bit: cast mask straight to f32 |\n| 4 | 67108864 | f16 | f32 | 0.5 | |\n| 5 | 67108864 | f32 | int8 | 2.0 | |\n| 6 | 1046529 | bf16 | uint8 | -1.0 | uint8 fixup |\n| 7 | 1030189 | f16 | int8 | 0.0 | mask range [0,127] — signed int8, no fixup |\n| 8 | 1181953 | f32 | f16 | 10.0 | |\n| 9 | 4977117 | bf16 | f32 | 1.5 | |\n| 10 | 1049337 | f16 | uint8 | 1.0 | mask [0,255], uint8 fixup |\n| 11 | 1093219 | f32 | int8 | 1.0 | 4-D |\n| 12 | 1000007 | bf16 | bf16 | inf | 1-D, inf propagation |\n| 13 | 10521419 | f32 | f32 | nan | 5-D, nan propagation |\n| 14 | 3074517 | f16 | int8 | 1.0 | x all zeros, 5-D |\n| 15 | 1049088 | f32 | uint8 | 1.0 | uint8 fixup |\n| 16 | 2089215 | bf16 | int8 | 1.2 | |\n| 17 | 2093057 | f16 | uint8 | -0.5 | uint8 fixup |\n| 18 | 2100222 | f32 | f16 | 0.75 | 3-D |\n| 19 | 2099232 | bf16 | f32 | 3.0 | 3-D |\n| 20 | 10554624 | f32 | int8 | 1.0 | 5-D |\n\nuint8 cases: 2, 6, 10, 15, 17 — host `mask.view(torch.int8)`, pass `is_uint8=1`\nflag, kernel applies +256 fixup in f32 via `asc2.where`.\n\n## Tiling & tails\n\n- 1-D grid-stride: `for t in asc2.range(block_idx, num_tiles, block_num, unroll_factor=2)`\n- Tail: `n = TILE if off + TILE <= size else size - off`; `real_shape=[n]` on\n  both copy_in and copy_out.\n- Two kernel variants dispatched by host:\n  - `_masked_scale_kernel` — normal (int8 or float mask, no uint8 fixup)\n  - `_masked_scale_kernel_uint8` — adds `asc2.where(mf < 0.0, mf + 256.0, mf)` after cast\n- Both accept `is_uint8` as `asc.ConstExpr[int]`? No — simpler: two separate\n  `@asc2.jit` functions to avoid branching cost on every tile.\n\n## UB budget\n\n| f32 tile | bytes per | count | subtotal |\n|----------|-----------|-------|----------|\n| xf | 4*TILE | 1 | 4T |\n| mf (after cast) | 4*TILE | 1 | 4T |\n| product xf*mf | 4*TILE | 1 | 4T |\n| scaled (xf*mf*scale) | 4*TILE | 1 | 4T |\n| where-fixup tile | 4*TILE | 1 (uint8 only) | 4T |\n| **visible @ TILE=2048** | | | 32768 (normal) / 40960 (uint8) |\n| **x1.6 measured** | | | ~52428 / ~65536 |\n| **x2 unroll** | | | ~104857 / ~131072 |\n\nBudget 253952 B. TILE=2048 fits comfortably for both variants.\n\n## Numerical risks\n\n- **inf/nan** (cases 12, 13): IEEE `*` propagates correctly — no special casing.\n- **Zero scale** (case 7): `0.0 * anything = 0.0` in IEEE, even with nan input\n  — golden uses same semantics, no issue.\n- **Large mask values** (case 10 mask up to 255 via uint8): f32 represents\n  exactly, no precision loss.\n- **f16/bf16 mixed** (cases 3, 6, 16): both 16-bit tiles are ONLY cast to f32,\n  never touched by arithmetic or comparisons at 16-bit.\n- **Precision thresholds**: f16 needs MERE < 2^-10 (~9.8e-4), f32 < 2^-13, bf16\n  < 2^-7. Single f32 multiply chain is exact for int8/uint8 masks and within\n  1 ulp for float masks — well within all thresholds.\n\n## Anti-cheat constraints\n\n- All compute in `@asc2.jit` kernels — no torch arithmetic on tensors.\n- torch used only for: `torch.empty_like(x)` allocation, `.numel()`, `.shape`,\n  `.dtype`, `.contiguous()`, `.view(torch.int8)` for uint8 reinterpret.\n- `ensure_npu_platform()` called first.\n- Output is contiguous, same shape and dtype as x.\n\n## Local validation ladder\n\n1. `python3 -m py_compile candidate.py` — syntax gate.\n2. Worker static contract check — callable signature, imports, anti-cheat.\n3. exact-v2 compile gate: all 20 dtype/constexpr combos must JIT-compile via\n   pinned pyasc v2 (`verified-local-compile`).\n4. No numerical claim until camodel or CANNBench evaluation.\n\n## Host dispatch pseudocode\n\n```\ndef masked_scale(x, mask, scale=1.0):\n    ensure_npu_platform()\n    x = x.contiguous(); mask = mask.contiguous()\n    out = torch.empty_like(x)\n    size = x.numel()\n    if size == 0: return out\n\n    is_uint8 = mask.dtype == torch.uint8\n    if is_uint8:\n        mask = mask.view(torch.int8)   # no-copy reinterpret\n\n    TILE = 2048\n    num_tiles = ceildiv(size, TILE)\n    cores = min(72, num_tiles)\n\n    if is_uint8:\n        _masked_scale_uint8[cores](x, mask, out, size, num_tiles, scale, TILE)\n    else:\n        _masked_scale_normal[cores](x, mask, out, size, num_tiles, scale, TILE)\n    return out\n```\n\nDESIGN_DONE","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":129,"totalLines":129,"truncated":false}},"title":"integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/masked_scale-generate/design.md","time":{"start":1788348082589,"end":1788348082595}},"id":"prt_061da2815001D4ulFSkeqdnLU4","sessionID":"ses_f9e260b29ffeaMqkDKkEFk3yuW","messageID":"msg_061da18a8001RPEMDLA3ofuKvN"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1788348083083,"sessionID":"ses_f9e260b29ffeaMqkDKkEFk3yuW","part":{"id":"prt_061da2596001qKuTcqbUENLijS","messageID":"msg_061da18a8001RPEMDLA3ofuKvN","sessionID":"ses_f9e260b29ffeaMqkDKkEFk3yuW","type":"text","text":"Now I'll read the three task files in parallel.","time":{"start":1788348081558,"end":1788348083082}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788348083089,"sessionID":"ses_f9e260b29ffeaMqkDKkEFk3yuW","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_dad9421234f8426f8db1e2ff","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/masked_scale-generate/candidate.py"},"output":"<path>/home/aloschilov/workspace/pyasc-skill-stack/integrations/cannbench/workers/.scratch/20260902_140812/masked_scale-generate/candidate.py</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: \"\"\"CANN Bench MaskedScale interface implemented as a pyasc asc2 kernel.\n2: \n3: y = x * mask * scale, elementwise.  Three kernel variants handle the\n4: independent dtype axes:\n5:   - _masked_scale_float_kernel  : float masks (f16 / bf16 / f32)\n6:   - _masked_scale_int8_kernel   : signed int8 masks (cast hop via f16)\n7:   - _masked_scale_uint8_kernel  : unsigned int8 masks (host view as int8,\n8:                                   +256 fixup in f32 via asc2.where)\n9: All compute is f32 inside the kernel; output is cast back to x's dtype.\n10: 1-D grid-stride tiling with TILE=2048 and unroll_factor=2.\n11: \"\"\"\n12: \n13: import torch\n14: \n15: import asc\n16: import asc2\n17: \n18: from ._pyasc_runtime import ensure_npu_platform\n19: \n20: _TILE = 2048\n21: _MAX_CORES = 72\n22: \n23: \n24: @asc2.jit\n25: def _masked_scale_float_kernel(\n26:     x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, mask_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress,\n27:     out_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, size: int, num_tiles: int,\n28:     scale: float, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int],\n29: ):\n30:     x_gm = asc2.global_tensor(x_ptr, [size])\n31:     mask_gm = asc2.global_tensor(mask_ptr, [size])\n32:     out_gm = asc2.global_tensor(out_ptr, [size])\n33:     for t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(),\n34:                         unroll_factor=2):\n35:         off = t * tile_size\n36:         n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off\n37:         x_tile = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n38:         m_tile = asc2.copy_in(mask_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n39:         xf = x_tile.to(asc.float32)\n40:         mf = m_tile.to(asc.float32)\n41:         y = xf * mf * scale\n42:         asc2.copy_out(y.to(x_tile.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n43: \n44: \n45: @asc2.jit\n46: def _masked_scale_int8_kernel(\n47:     x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, mask_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress,\n48:     out_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, size: int, num_tiles: int,\n49:     scale: float, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int],\n50: ):\n51:     x_gm = asc2.global_tensor(x_ptr, [size])\n52:     mask_gm = asc2.global_tensor(mask_ptr, [size])\n53:     out_gm = asc2.global_tensor(out_ptr, [size])\n54:     for t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(),\n55:                         unroll_factor=2):\n56:         off = t * tile_size\n57:         n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off\n58:         x_tile = asc2.copy_in(x_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n59:         m_tile = asc2.copy_in(mask_gm, [off], [tile_size], real_shape=[n])\n60:         xf = x_tile.to(asc.float32)\n61:         mf = asc2.cast(m_tile, asc.float16).to(asc.float32)\n62:         y = xf * mf * scale\n63:         asc2.copy_out(y.to(x_tile.dtype), out_gm, [off], real_shape=[n])\n64: \n65: \n66: @asc2.jit\n67: def _masked_scale_uint8_kernel(\n68:     x_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, mask_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress,\n69:     out_ptr: asc.GlobalAddress, size: int, num_tiles: int,\n70:     scale: float, tile_size: asc.ConstExpr[int],\n71: ):\n72:     x_gm = asc2.global_tensor(x_ptr, [size])\n73:     mask_gm = asc2.global_tensor(mask_ptr, [size])\n74:     out_gm = asc2.global_tensor(out_ptr, [size])\n75:     for t in asc2.range(asc2.block_idx(), num_tiles, asc2.block_num(),\n76:                         unroll_factor=2):\n77:         off = t * tile_size\n78:         n = tile_size if off + tile_size <= size else size - off\n79:         x